# Causal Networks

## Causal DAGs

// ## 13.5 Causal networks (p. 462)

* (Non-Causal) Bayesian Networks
- @Definition@: **Bayesian networks** represent a joint distribution function
  - The direction of the arrow represent _conditional dependence_ (not
    causality)
  - An edge $A \to B$ requires to estimate $\Pr(A | B)$

- @Fact@: **Several possible Bayesian networks** to explain the same phenomenon
  can have same nodes, but different edges 
  - Are _equivalent_ and convey the same information
  - Have different difficulties to be estimated

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}

- @Example@: A Bayesian network with $Fire$ and $Smoke$ (which are dependent)
  - $Fire \to Smoke$
    - Need $\Pr(Fire)$ and $\Pr(Smoke | Fire)$ to compute $\Pr(Fire, Smoke)$
  - $Smoke \to Fire$
    - Need $\Pr(Smoke)$ and $\Pr(Fire | Smoke)$
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianNetwork1 {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Fire [label="P(Fire)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Smoke [label="P(Smoke)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    Fire -> Smoke [label="Pr(Smoke | Fire)"];
}
```

\vspace{1.5cm}

```graphviz
digraph BayesianNetwork1 {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Fire [label="Fire", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Smoke [label="Smoke", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    Smoke -> Fire [label="Pr(Fire | Smoke)"];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Key idea@: **Asymmetry in nature** reveals causality
  - Extinguishing fire stops smoke
  - Clearing smoke doesn't affect fire

* Causal (Bayesian) Networks
- @Definition@: **Causal networks** are Bayesian networks with _only_ causal
  edges
  - Use common sense and domain expertise instead of only statistics
  - Go from _"Are $Smoke$ and $Fire$ correlated?"_ to _"What causes what?"_

- @Intuition@: **"Dependency in nature"** is like assignment in programming
  - Nature assigns $Smoke$ based on $Fire$: $Smoke := f(Fire)$
  - Not $Fire := f(Smoke)$

- @Definition@: **Structural equations** describe causal mechanisms
  $$
  X_i := f(X_j) \iff X_j \to X_i
  $$

* Causal DAG
- @Property@: **Causal DAG** has two key properties
  - _Directed_: Arrows show cause $\to$ effect
  - _Acyclic_: No feedback loops (causality assumes temporal order)

- @Pros@
  - Make explicit _causal_ links
  - Support explainable models
  - Stable conditional probability estimation
  - Reason about interventions and counterfactuals

- @Challenges@
  - Requires domain knowledge for structure
  - Assumes all relevant variables included (no hidden confounders)

* Causal Edges are Stable

- @Characteristics@: Causal edges reflect **stable relationships** across
  contexts

  - _Mechanistic stability_
    - Causal relationships show system function, not just dataset behavior
    - E.g., _"Temperature $\to$ ice melting"_ holds in Alaska and Arizona

  - _Invariance under interventions_
    - If $X$ causes $Y$, intervening on $X$ affects $Y$ consistently
    - Despite confounders or context changes

- @Example@: _Exercise $\to$ Health_ remains stable across datasets
  - Correlation values may differ in young or elderly populations
  - Causal effect stable because physiological mechanism doesn't change

* Causal DAG: Example
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **Explanatory variables** (aka treatment) can be manipulated or
  observed when changes applied
  - E.g., _"does coffee before exam help test score?"_
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph SCMExample {
  rankdir=TB;
  splines=true;
  nodesep=0.5;
  ranksep=0.5;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

  Coffee [label="Large Cup of Coffee", fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
  Test_Score [label="Test Score Improvement", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
  Room_Temp [label="Room Temperature", fillcolor="#decbe4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];

  Coffee -> Test_Score;
  Room_Temp -> Test_Score [style=dashed];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Definition@: **Outcome variables** (aka effect) is the result of the action
  - E.g., _"by how much did test score improve?"_

- @Definition@: **Unobserved variables** (latent/hidden) is not measured or
  difficult to account
  - E.g., _"room temperature makes students sleepy"_

### Ladder of Causation: Example

* Example: Tornado Warning
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Consider a **severe weather alert system**
  - The _causal diagram_ has the following nodes and structure:
    - $T$: _"Tornado forms"_
    - $W$: _"Tornado warning is issued"_
    - $A, B$: _"Radio and TV broadcast emergency"_
    - $R$: _"Residents are warned"_

- @Assumptions@
  - Channels broadcast only on official order
  - Delivery systems never fail
  - If either channel activates, then the residents warned
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph Tornado_Warning {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    // Node styles
    T [label="Tornado forms (T)", fillcolor="#FFB3BA"]; // pastel red
    W [label="Tornado warning (W)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"]; // pastel orange
    A [label="Radio broadcasts (A)", fillcolor="#FFF4A3"]; // pastel yellow
    B [label="TV broadcasts (B)", fillcolor="#CFECCF"];   // pastel green
    R [label="Residents warned (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]; // pastel blue

    // Force ranks (top to bottom)
    { rank=min;  T; }
    { rank=same; W; }
    { rank=same; A; B; }
    { rank=max;  R; }

    T -> W;
    W -> A;
    W -> B;
    A -> R;
    B -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Goal@: We want to look at this examples using the ladder of causation
  - Association $\to$ intevention $\to$ counterfactual

* Tornado Warning: Level 1 (Association)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **Association** (observational quantity) understands how one fact informs
  another

- @Question@: _If residents warned, was warning issued?_
  - Does $R \implies W$? Yes

- @Question@: _If radio $A$ broadcast, did TV $B$ also?_
  - Yes, true even though $A$ doesn't cause $B$
  - $A$ and $B$ are correlated through common cause $W$ (aka confounder)
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph Tornado_Warning {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    // Node styles
    T [label="Tornado forms (T)", fillcolor="#FFB3BA"]; // pastel red
    W [label="Tornado warning (W)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"]; // pastel orange
    A [label="Radio broadcasts (A)", fillcolor="#FFF4A3"]; // pastel yellow
    B [label="TV broadcasts (B)", fillcolor="#CFECCF"];   // pastel green
    R [label="Residents warned (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]; // pastel blue

    // Force ranks (top to bottom)
    { rank=min;  T; }
    { rank=same; W; }
    { rank=same; A; B; }
    { rank=max;  R; }

    T -> W;
    W -> A;
    W -> B;
    A -> R;
    B -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

* Tornado Warning: Level 2 (Intervention)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Question@: _If you make radio broadcast, will residents be warned?_
  - Yes
  - Breaks "rules of nature" as $A$ broadcasts only if $W$ is issued
    - Like removing edge $W \to A$ in graph
    - Set $A$ to _true_: $do(A=1)$
  - Expect $B$ did _not_ broadcast
    - Forcing $A$ shouldn't affect $B$ (no $A \to B$ edge)
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph Tornado_Warning {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    // Node styles
    T [label="Tornado forms (T)", fillcolor="#FFB3BA"]; // pastel red
    W [label="Tornado warning (W)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"]; // pastel orange
    A [
      label="Radio broadcasts (A)",
      fillcolor="#FFF4A3",
      xlabel=<<B><FONT COLOR="red">do(A = 1)</FONT></B>>
    ]; // Orange
    B [label="TV broadcasts (B)", fillcolor="#CFECCF"];   // pastel green
    R [label="Residents warned (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]; // pastel blue

    // Force ranks (top to bottom)
    { rank=min;  T; }
    { rank=same; W; }
    { rank=same; A; B; }
    { rank=max;  R; }

    T -> W;
    W -> A [style=invis];
    W -> B;
    A -> R;
    B -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Key idea@: Difference between **seeing and doing**
  - _See_ $A$ broadcast $\implies$ conclude $B$ broadcast too
  - _Make_ $A$ broadcast $\implies$ expect $B$ didn't

- @Key idea@: Observational **data alone doesn't reveal causality**
  - Either all variables $T,W,A,B,R$ true or all false
  - Correlations alone uninformative

* Tornado Warning: Level 3 (Counterfactual)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **Counterfactual** reasoning
  - Assume residents warned: $R=1$
  - Know $A, B$ broadcast because $W$ issued, after $T$ formed

- @Question@: _Would residents be warned if radio $A$ didn't broadcast?_
  - Remove edge into $A$, set $do(A=0)$
  - TV $B$ still broadcasts (due to $W$)
  - $R=(A \lor B)$ remains true

- @Answer@
  - Yes, $R=1$
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph Tornado_Warning {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    edge [penwidth=1.2, color="#555555", arrowsize=0.9];

    // Node styles
    T [label="Tornado forms (T)", fillcolor="#FFB3BA"]; // pastel red
    W [label="Tornado warning (W)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"]; // pastel orange
    A [
      label="Radio broadcasts (A)",
      fillcolor="#FFF4A3",
      xlabel=<<B><FONT COLOR="red">do(A = 0)</FONT></B>>
    ]; // Orange
    B [label="TV broadcasts (B)", fillcolor="#CFECCF"];   // pastel green
    R [label="Residents warned (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]; // pastel blue

    // Force ranks (top to bottom)
    { rank=min;  T; }
    { rank=same; W; }
    { rank=same; A; B; }
    { rank=max;  R; }

    T -> W;
    W -> A [style=invis];
    W -> B;
    A -> R;
    B -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

## Structural Causal Model

* Structural Causal Model
- @Definition@: **Structural Causal Model** (SCM)
  - Translates causal DAG into mathematical equations
  - Defines how variables interact

- @Components@
  - _Variables_ $X_1, X_2, ..., X_n$ represent system quantities
  - _Equations_ model each as function of direct causes
    $$X_i = f_i(Parents(X_i), \varepsilon_i)$$
    where:
    - $Parents(X_i)$ are direct causes
    - $\varepsilon_i$ is noise

- @Properties@
  - Explain causal relationships
  - Quantify causal effects
  - Foundation for reasoning and simulation

- @Remark@: Used in econometrics/genetics long before modern causal theory

* Structural Causal Model: Sprinkler Example
::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: _Structural equations for sprinkle example_
// TODO(ai_gp): Why is not rendered correctly.
  $$
  \begin{cases}
  C := f_C(\varepsilon_C) \\
  R := f_R(C, \varepsilon_R) \\
  S := f_S(C, \varepsilon_S) \\
  W := f_W(R, S, \varepsilon_W) \\
  G := f_G(W, \varepsilon_G)
  \end{cases}
  $$

- @Definition@: **Exogenous noise** $\varepsilon_x$ are unobserved,
  independent with prior distribution
  - E.g., error terms like $\varepsilon_W$ = other wetness sources ($MorningDew$)

::::
:::: {.column width=25%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    rankdir=TD;
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Cloudy        [label="Cloudy",        fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    Sprinkler     [label="Sprinkler",     fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    Rain          [label="Rain",          fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    WetGrass      [label="Wet Grass",     fillcolor="#decbe4"];
    GreenerGrass  [label="Greener Grass", fillcolor="#fed9a6"];

    // Force ranks
    { rank=same; Sprinkler; Rain; }

    Cloudy -> Sprinkler;
    Cloudy -> Rain;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
    Rain -> WetGrass;
    WetGrass -> GreenerGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Remark@: _Joint distribution_ from causal DAG topology
  \begingroup \small
  $$
  \Pr(C, R, S, W, G)
  = \Pr(W|R,S) \cdot \Pr(G|W) \cdot \Pr(S|C) \cdot \Pr(R|C) \cdot \Pr(C)
  $$
  \endgroup

## Variables

* Observed Vs. Unobserved Variables
::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Definition@: **Observed variables** (measurable/visible) are directly measured
  or collected in dataset
  - E.g., Education, Income

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph EducationIncomeModel_ObservedUnobserved_Vertical {
    rankdir=TD; // top-down layout
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Education       [label="Education",       fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    Income          [label="Income",          fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
    EconomicPolicy  [label="Economic Policy", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];

    Motivation      [label="Motivation",      fillcolor="#decbe4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    NaturalTalent   [label="Natural Talent",  fillcolor="#decbe4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    Weather         [label="Weather",         fillcolor="#decbe4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];
    News            [label="News",            fillcolor="#decbe4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];

    // Vertical order (top to bottom)
    { rank=same; EconomicPolicy; }
    { rank=same; NaturalTalent; }
    { rank=same; Education; }
    { rank=same; Motivation; }
    { rank=same; News; Weather; }
    { rank=same; Income; }

    // Causal links
    EconomicPolicy  -> Education;
    EconomicPolicy  -> Income;
    NaturalTalent   -> Education;
    NaturalTalent   -> Motivation;
    Motivation      -> Education;
    Motivation      -> Income;
    Education       -> Income;
    News            -> Income;

    // Legend (top, vertical stack)
    subgraph cluster_legend {
        label="Legend";
        fontsize=11;
        color="#dddddd";
        style="rounded";
        rankdir=TB; // vertical stacking

        key1 [label="Observed", shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#ccebc5"];
        key2 [label="Unobserved",  shape=box, style="rounded,filled,dashed", fillcolor="#decbe4"];

        key1 -> key2 [style=invis];
    }
}
```
::::
:::

- @Definition@: **Unobserved variables** (latent/hidden) exist but not measured
  or included in data
  - E.g., Natural talent, Motivation
  - Ignoring them leads to spurious conclusions
    - E.g., $IceCreamSales \leftarrow Temperature \to DrowningRates$

* Endogenous Vs. Exogenous Variables
::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Definition@: **Endogenous variables** have their values determined _within_
  model
  - Dependent on other system variables
  - Represent internal behavior/outcomes
  - E.g., Motivation, Income

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph EducationIncomeModel_EndoExo{
    rankdir=TD; // top-down layout
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Education       [label="Education",       fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    Income          [label="Income",          fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    EconomicPolicy  [label="Economic Policy", fillcolor="#fed9a6"];
    Motivation      [label="Motivation",      fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
    NaturalTalent   [label="Natural Talent",  fillcolor="#fed9a6"];
    Weather         [label="Weather",         fillcolor="#fed9a6"];
    News            [label="News",            fillcolor="#fed9a6"];

    // Vertical order (top to bottom)
    { rank=same; EconomicPolicy; }
    { rank=same; NaturalTalent; }
    { rank=same; Education; }
    { rank=same; Motivation; }
    { rank=same; News; Weather; }
    { rank=same; Income; }

    // Causal links
    EconomicPolicy  -> Education;
    EconomicPolicy  -> Income;
    NaturalTalent   -> Education;
    NaturalTalent   -> Motivation;
    Motivation      -> Education;
    Motivation      -> Income;
    Education       -> Income;
    News            -> Income;

    // Legend at top
    subgraph cluster_legend {
        label="Legend";
        fontsize=11;
        color="#dddddd";
        style="rounded";
        // Vertical stacking.
        rankdir=TB;

        key1 [label="Endogenous", shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#b3cde3"];
        key2 [label="Exogenous",  shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#fed9a6"];

        key1 -> key2 [style=invis];
    }
}
```
::::
:::
- @Definition@: **Exogenous variables**
  - Originate _outside_ model
  - Not caused by other system variables
  - Represent background conditions/external shocks
  - E.g., Natural talent, Economic policy, Weather, News

* Endo / Exogenous, Observed / Unobserved Vars
::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
- @Characteristics@:
  - _Exogenous + unobserved_: capture randomness, unknown factors
  - _Exogenous + observed_: potential intervention targets
  - _Endogenous + observed_: focus for prediction, intervention

::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

