// TODO(ai_gp): Convert to typst
\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Variable Type**        | **Observability**   | **Example**          |
|---------------------------|---------------------|---------------------|
| Endogenous               | Observed            | Income               |
| Exogenous                | Observed            | Education            |
| Endogenous               | Unobserved          | Motivation           |
| Exogenous                | Unobserved          | Natural Talent       |
\endgroup

::::
:::

* Building a Causal DAG
- Causal models **visually represent systems**
  - Nodes ("nouns"): price, sales, revenue, weight, period, etc.
  - Variables can be endogenous/exogenous, observed/unobserved
  - Edges ("verbs"): Parents, children, descendants, ancestors, neighbors

- **Modeling as communication tool**
  - Shared language bridges technical and non-technical team members
  - Iterative refinement: continuously update with new variables and insights

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Question@: What is the relationship between stress and heart attacks?

- @Answer@: Causal DAG with nodes and edges
  - Treatment: _Stress_
  - Outcome: _Heart attack_
  - Stress not direct cause; acts through Diet and Exercise
  - Unobserved: _Genetics_
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph CausalGraph {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Stress [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Diet [fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Exercise [fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    Heart_Attack [label="Heart Attack", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    Genetic [fillcolor="#A6E7F4", style="rounded,filled,dashed"];

    Stress -> Diet;
    Stress -> Exercise;
    Diet -> Heart_Attack;
    Exercise -> Heart_Attack;
    Genetic -> Heart_Attack;
}
```
::::
:::

* Weights
- @Definition@: **Edge weights** represent causal strength
  - Sign indicates direction of interaction:
  -E.g., positive means that an increase in source variable implies an increase in
   the value of the destination variable

```graphviz
digraph RiskFactors {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Age [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Gender [fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Blood_Pressure [fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    Cholesterol [fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    Exercise [fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Heart_Attack [label="Heart Attach", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    Age -> Heart_Attack [label="+0.8"];
    Gender -> Heart_Attack [label="+0.3"];
    Blood_Pressure -> Heart_Attack [label="+0.7"];
    Cholesterol -> Heart_Attack [label="+0.5"];
    Exercise -> Heart_Attack [label="-0.6"];
}
```

- Relationships can be estimated in several ways:
  - Correlation-based
  - Bayesian approach with priors

## Type of Variables in Causal AI

* Mediator Variable
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **Mediator variable** $M$ is an intermediate variable that
  _transmits_ causal effect from $X$ to $Y$
  - Lies on causal path between $X$ and $Y$
  - Captures mechanism through which $X$ influences $Y$

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph CausalFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Treatment\n(X)"];
    M [fillcolor="#F4A6A6", label="Mediator\n(M)"];
    Y [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Outcome\n(Y)"];

    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Remark@: **Direct vs. indirect effects**
  - Indirect: $X$ affects $Y$ through $M$
  - Direct: $X$ affects $Y$ not through $M$
  - Controlling for $M$ separates effects
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph CausalFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Treatment\n(X)"];
    M [fillcolor="#F4A6A6", label="Mediator\n(M)"];
    Y [fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Outcome\n(Y)"];

    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
    X -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

* Mediator Variable: Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- @Question@: Does training increase employee productivity?

- @Answer@: There is an indirect causal effect through mediator
  - $Training$ doesn't immediately boost $Productivity$
  - Enhances $Job satisfaction$ $\implies$ raises $Productivity$
  - Path: $X \to M \to Y$
    - $X$: Training Program
    - $M$: Job Satisfaction (mediator)
    - $Y$: Employee Productivity

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz
digraph CausalFlow {
    rankdir=LR;
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="Training program (X)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", color=black];
    M [label="Job satisfaction (M)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6", color=black];
    Y [label="Employee productivity (Y)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", color=black];

    // Force ranks
    { rank=same; X; M; Y; }

    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
    X -> Y [style=dashed];
}
```
::::
:::

* Moderator Variable
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: A **moderator variable** $M$ changes _strength_ or _direction_ of
  relationship between $X$ and $Y$
  - Not part of causal chain, but _conditions_ the relationship
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph ModeratorModel {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="Treatment\n(X)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", pos="0,1!"];
    Y [label="Outcome\n(Y)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", pos="2,1!"];
    M [label="Moderator\n(M)", pos="1,0!", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    XY [label="", shape=point, width=0.01, style=invis, pos="1,1!"];

    X -> XY [arrowhead=none];
    XY -> Y;
    M -> XY;

    // Use neato for fixed positioning
    layout=neato;
  }
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Question@: What is the relationship between stress and job performance?

- @Answer@: Social support acts as a moderator
  - High support weakens stress's negative effect
  - Low support strengthens stress's negative effect
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph ModeratorModel {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [label="Stress (X)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", pos="0,1!"];
    Y [label="Job\nPerformance (M)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4", pos="2,1!"];
    M [label="Social Support (Y)", pos="1,0!", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    XY [label="", shape=circle, width=0.01, style=invis, pos="1,1!"];

    X -> XY [arrowhead=none];
    XY -> Y;
    M -> XY;

    // Use neato for fixed positioning
    layout=neato;
}
```
::::
:::

* Confounder Variable
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Definition@: **Confounder** $C$ is a variable that affects both treatment
  (cause) and outcome (effect)
  - Creates spurious association if not controlled

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph CausalTriangle {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Confounder [label="Confounder (C)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Treatment  [label="Treatment (X)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Outcome    [label="Outcome (Y)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    Confounder -> Treatment;
    Confounder -> Outcome;
    Treatment  -> Outcome;

    {rank=same; Treatment; Outcome;}
}
```
::::
:::

* Confounder Variable: Example
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Example@: _Ice cream sales and drowning correlate_
  - Is it causality?

- Spurious explanations abound
  - E.g., distraction, sugar spike, hyperventilation, etc.
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```tikz
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={Ice cream sales},
    ylabel={Drownings},
    width=10cm,
    height=6cm,
    axis lines=left,
    xtick=\empty,
    ytick=\empty,
    enlargelimits=true,
    line width=0.9pt,
    title={Observed association}
]
% rising trend due to common cause "Summer"
\addplot+[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=blue!60] coordinates {
    (0.3,0.2) (0.8,0.7) (1.2,1.1) (1.6,1.6)
    (2.1,2.4) (2.8,3.0) (3.4,3.6) (4.2,4.7) (5.1,6.0) (6.0,7.8)
};
% visual cue of spurious linear fit
%\addplot[samples=2,domain=0.2:6.2, very thick, color=red!60, dashed] {1.25*x-0.9};
\end{axis}
```

```graphviz
digraph CausalTriangle {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    IceCreamSales [label="IceCreamSales", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Drowning [label="Drowning", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];

    {rank=same; IceCreamSales; Drowning;}

    IceCreamSales -> Drowning;
    Drowning -> IceCreamSales;
}
```
::::
:::


- @Question@: How to act on this relationship?
  - Ban ice cream to reduce drowning?
  - Suspicious reasoning hides confounding

* Confounder Variable: Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- @Key idea@: **No cause-effect** between $IceCreamSales$ and $Drowning$
  - $Temperature$ is the confounder

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph CausalTriangle {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Temperature [label="Temperature", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    IceCreamSales [label="IceCreamSales", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Drowning [label="Drowning", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];

    {rank=same; IceCreamSales; Drowning;}

    Temperature -> IceCreamSales;
    Temperature -> Drowning;
}
```

::::
:::

- In fact controlling for temperature removes association

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

```tikz
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={Ice cream sales},
    ylabel={Drownings},
    width=10cm,
    height=6cm,
    axis lines=left,
    xtick=\empty,
    ytick=\empty,
    enlargelimits=true,
    line width=0.9pt,
    title={After controlling for temperature}
]
% group 1: low temperature days
\addplot+[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=blue!60] coordinates {
    (0.3,0.2) (0.8,0.3) (1.2,0.2) (1.6,0.4)
};
% group 2: medium temperature days
\addplot+[only marks, mark=triangle*, mark size=2pt, color=orange!70] coordinates {
    (2.1,1.1) (2.8,1.2) (3.4,1.0) (3.9,1.1)
};
% group 3: hot days
\addplot+[only marks, mark=square*, mark size=2pt, color=red!70] coordinates {
    (4.2,2.1) (5.1,2.0) (6.0,2.2) (6.5,2.1)
};
\node[anchor=south east, font=\small, text=black!70] at (rel axis cs:1.0,0.05)
{No trend within each temperature band};
\end{axis}
```

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```tikz
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={Ice Cream Sales},
    ylabel={Drownings},
    width=11cm,
    height=6.5cm,
    axis lines=left,
    xtick=\empty,
    ytick=\empty,
    enlargelimits=true,
    line width=0.9pt,
    ymin=0,
    ymax=2.5,
    title={No relationship once temperature is controlled}
]
% Cool days (low temperature)
\addplot+[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt, color=blue!60] coordinates {
    (0.3,0.2) (0.8,0.3) (1.2,0.2) (1.6,0.4) (2.1,0.3) (2.8,0.2) (3.4,0.4) (3.9,0.5) (4.2,0.4) (5.1,0.3) (6.0,0.2) (6.5,0.2)
};
\node[anchor=south east, font=\small, text=black!70] at (rel axis cs:0.95,0.08)
{Flat: no causal effect};
\end{axis}
```
::::
:::

* Collider
::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **Collider** $A$ is a variable influenced by multiple sources
  $B$, $C$
  - Complicates reasoning about relationships $B, C \to Y$
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
```graphviz
digraph ColliderDiagram {
  layout=dot;
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

  B [label="Cause 1 (B)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
  C [label="Cause 2 (C)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
  A [label="Collider (A)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
  Y [label="Effect (Y)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

  B -> A;
  C -> A;
  A -> Y;

  {rank=same; B; C;}
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: Exercise vs. Heart Disease
  - $Diet$ and $Exercise$ influence $Body Weight$
  - $Body Weight$ influences $Heart Disease$
  - $Body Weight$ is collider
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz
digraph ColliderDiagram {
  layout=dot;
  rankdir=TB;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

  E [label="Exercise (E)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]
  D [label="Diet (D)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]
  W [label="Body Weight (W)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
  H [label="Heart Disease (H)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

  E -> W;
  D -> W;
  W -> H;

  {rank=same; E; D;}
}
```
::::
:::

* Collider Bias
- @Definition@: **Collider bias** (aka Berkson's paradox)
  - Conditioning on collider opens blocked path
  - Introduces spurious association between parents

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: Consider the causal graph with the following variables
  - $D$ = Diet, $E$ = Exercise, $W$ = Body Weight, $H$ = Heart Disease

- _Without conditioning on $W$_
  - $E$ and $D$ independent
  - Knowing $E$ doesn't inform $D$
  - Collider $W$ blocks association
::::
:::: {.column width=25%}
```graphviz
digraph ColliderDiagram {
  layout=dot;
  rankdir=TB;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

  E [label="Exercise (E)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]
  D [label="Diet (D)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"]
  W [label="Body Weight (W)", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
  H [label="Heart Disease (H)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

  E -> W;
  D -> W;
  W -> H;

  {rank=same; E; D;}
}
```
::::
:::

- _After conditioning on $W$_
  - Fix $W$ at specific value
  - Creates dependency between $E$ and $D$
  - Changes in $E$ balanced by changes in $D$
  - Induces spurious correlation (called "explaining away")

## Types of Paths in Causal AI

* Chain

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: **Chain** $X \to M \to Y$ is sequential causal path
  - $X$ affects $M$, which affects $Y$
  - Represents mediation or transmission of causal effect
  - $M$ is intermediate (mediator) variable

- _Conditioning on $M$ blocks path_ and interrupts causal effect
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph ChainStructure {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    M [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Y [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    X -> M;
    M -> Y;
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
- @Example@:
  - $Education$ influences $Income$, which influences $LifeQuality$
  - Education's effect on quality passes through income
  - Path: $Education \to Income \to LifeQuality$
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph EducationChain {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    Education [fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Income [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    LifeQuality [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Education -> Income;
    Income -> LifeQuality;
}
```
::::
:::


* Fork
::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: **Fork** $D \leftarrow X \to C$ is when a single variable $X$
  influences multiple targets
  - $X$ is confounder (common cause) of $C$ and $D$
  - Induces dependence even without direct link

- _Conditioning on $X$ blocks path_ and removes spurious correlation
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph CausalDAG {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    X [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    C [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    D [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    {rank=same; C; D;}

    X -> C;
    X -> D;
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Example@:
  - $Lifestyle$ confounds $Weight$ and $BloodPressure$
  - Outcomes correlated due to shared cause
::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph LifestyleFork {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    Lifestyle [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Weight [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    BloodPressure [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    {rank=same; Weight; BloodPressure;}
    Lifestyle -> Weight;
    Lifestyle -> BloodPressure;
}
```
::::
:::

* Inverted Fork

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: **Inverted fork** $B \to A \leftarrow C$ is when two or more
  arrows converge on common node
  - Colliders block associations (unless conditioned on)

- _Conditioning on collider opens path_ and induces spurious correlations
  - Basis of selection bias
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph ColliderStructure {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    B [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    A [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    C [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    {rank=same; B; C;}

    B -> A;
    C -> A;
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: **Sales collider**
  - $MarketingSpend$ and $ProductQuality$ influence $Sales$
  - Before observing sales: spend and quality independent
  - After conditioning on sales: spend and quality become dependent
  - High sales with low quality suggests high marketing spend
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz
digraph SalesCollider {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    Marketing [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Sales [fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Quality [fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    {rank=same; Marketing; Quality;}

    Marketing -> Sales;
    Quality -> Sales;
}
```
::::
:::
